<h1>     Exercise Name: Deficit Dumbbell Push-Up</h1></pre>
    <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
The video demonstrates the Deficit Dumbbell Push-Up (or Push-Up on Dumbbells). By gripping dumbbells placed on the floor instead of placing your hands flat, you elevate your hands relative to the ground. This creates a "deficit" that allows your chest to sink lower at the bottom of the movement, providing a deeper stretch and greater muscle engagement.

Target Muscle Activation

 Primary: Chest / Pectorals (~70%)
 Secondary: Anterior Deltoids / Shoulders (~30%) & Triceps Brachii

</div></div>
<pre>
    <h1>Step-by-Step Execution Guide</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>:
 Place two dumbbells on the floor parallel to each other, roughly shoulder-width apart. Hexagonal dumbbells work best to prevent rolling.
 Grip the handles with a neutral grip (palms facing each other) and extend your legs back into a high plank position.
 Keep your shoulders directly over your hands, core engaged, and body forming a straight line from head to heels.


<u><b><h4>2. The Descent (Eccentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Inhale and slowly lower your body toward the floor by bending your elbows.
 Keep your elbows tucked at roughly a 45-degree angle relative to your torso.
 Lower yourself below the height of the dumbbell handles until you feel a deep stretch across your chest muscles.


<u><b><h4>3. The Push (Concentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Exhale and press forcefully through your palms to return to the starting plank position.
 Keep your spine completely straight and lock out your arms controlledly at the top without rounding your upper back.





<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Deeper Range of Motion:</b> The elevated handles allow your chest to drop lower than a standard floor push-up, increasing time-under-tension and muscle hypertrophy.
 <b>◉Wrist-Friendly Grip:</b> Holding handles in a neutral position keeps your wrists straight, reducing joint strain often caused by flat-hand floor push-ups.
 <b>◉Enhanced Grip & Forearm Stability:</b> Gripping the handles tightly engages your forearms and demands extra wrist stabilization throughout the movement.
 <b>◉Stronger Chest Activation:</b> The added stretch at the bottom forces the pectoral muscles to work harder during the initial phase of the press.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

 Hypertrophy (Muscle Growth): 3–4 sets of 8–12 controlled reps.
 Bodyweight Endurance: 3 sets of 15–20 reps (or to near failure).
 Strength Progression: 3–4 sets of 6–10 reps (you can add a weighted vest for higher resistance).
 Rest: 60–90 seconds between sets.
